DIGITOID — COMPLETE TESTING FEEDBACK & UX REFINEMENT

Final Consolidated Implementation Prompt for Claude

You are working on the existing Digitoid project at ~/digitoid.

I have completed my initial hands-on testing and identified several functional, usability, and workflow improvements.

This document combines all feedback from the first testing review through the latest Findings screen review.

Treat this as the single source of truth for the current refinement pass. It supersedes the earlier individual testing-feedback prompts.

Do not redesign Digitoid from scratch.

Preserve the approved:

* Digitoid AI robot identity and branding.
* Boubyan complementary branding.
* Design system and typography.
* Four-tab review workspace.
* Six-stage engineering workflow.
* AI agent orchestration and team approval gates.
* Existing integrations and security boundaries.

The objective is to make Digitoid simpler, more organized, more intelligent, and easier for developers to use.

The main principle is:

Show developers what matters, guide them to the right place, and ask only for what is needed.

⸻

1. PROFESSIONAL SIDEBAR REDESIGN

The current sidebar needs to feel more professional and organized.

It should function as a focused engineering task workspace rather than a generic chat history.

1.1 Sidebar Structure

Organize the sidebar into:

1. Digitoid logo and wordmark.
2. Subtle Boubyan Digital Factory identity.
3. New Review action.
4. Review search.
5. Active Reviews.
6. Archived Reviews.
7. Account and Settings.

Follow the approved Digitoid design system.

Use restrained colors, consistent spacing, and clear typography.

Avoid:

* Excessive icons.
* Decorative elements.
* Unnecessary badges.
* Heavy borders.
* Repeated separators.
* Large empty areas.
* Excessive metadata.

The sidebar should remain usable with many reviews.

1.2 Active Reviews

Show reviews that still require work or attention.

Include:

* Running.
* Queued.
* Awaiting input.
* Paused.
* Stopped.
* Failed.
* Incomplete.

Each review should display:

* A meaningful feature title.
* One concise status description.

Reviews requiring developer action should be easy to identify.

Keep sorting predictable.

Use subtle highlighting for the selected review.

1.3 Meaningful Review Titles

Avoid repeated generic titles such as “New review.”

Use this fallback order:

1. Feature name from the generated report.
2. Feature name from intake.
3. First meaningful user message.
4. Neutral untitled-review label.

Do not create duplicate review entries.

Handle long titles gracefully.

1.4 Automatic Archiving

When a review is genuinely completed, automatically move it to Archived Reviews.

Archived Reviews must be collapsed and hidden by default.

A review qualifies for automatic archiving only when:

* The actual workflow has successfully completed.
* Required approval gates are resolved.
* Required final reports are generated.
* The execution plan is ready.
* No blocking clarification remains.

Do not archive reviews merely because the Claude process stopped.

Do not archive:

* Failed reviews.
* Stopped reviews.
* Incomplete reviews.
* Awaiting-input reviews.
* Queued reviews.

Archiving must not modify or delete review content.

Preserve:

* Findings.
* Clarifications.
* Responses.
* Execution plans.
* Generated reports.
* Conversation history.
* Review metadata.

If a review completes while open, keep it visible in the workspace even though it moves into the archive.

Do not abruptly navigate away.

1.5 Archived Reviews

* Collapsed by default.
* Show archived review count.
* Expandable when needed.
* Searchable even while collapsed.
* Fully accessible.
* No mandatory manual Archive action.
* No automatic reopening after navigation.

1.6 Search

Search by review title.

Include both active and archived reviews.

Show archived matches in search results without requiring users to expand the archive.

Keep search fast and simple.

Avoid unnecessary advanced filters.

1.7 Delete Review

Add a discreet contextual menu to sidebar review rows.

Support Delete Review with confirmation.

Requirements:

* Clearly identify the review.
* Require explicit confirmation.
* Handle active sessions safely.
* Prevent partial deletion.
* Respect filesystem and security boundaries.
* Remove associated data according to the existing lifecycle.
* Update the sidebar after successful deletion.
* Navigate safely if the selected review is deleted.
* Never affect unrelated reviews.

Do not implement deletion as a visual-only operation.

