8. FINDINGS SCREEN — COMPLETE SIMPLIFICATION

The Findings screen is currently too technical and difficult to scan.

It exposes internal report identifiers, classifications, source filenames, and lengthy descriptions.

The redesigned screen must answer:

Who needs to do what, and why?

Use extremely simple, natural English.

Do not make users interpret internal AI terminology.

8.1 Organize by Responsible Team

Replace the current primary categories with:

* All
* Product Owner
* Design Team
* Developer

The All view should be the default.

In All, group findings by responsible team.

Product Owner

Business-related items, including:

* Missing business rules.
* Conflicting requirements.
* Unclear behavior.
* Acceptance criteria.
* Business decisions.

Design Team

Design-related items, including:

* Figma/BRD mismatches.
* Missing screens.
* Missing UI states.
* Unclear journeys.
* Design decisions.

Developer

Engineering-related items, including:

* Existing code reuse.
* Technical dependencies.
* API questions.
* Implementation risks.
* Technical clarifications.

Use actual owner metadata whenever available.

Do not automatically assign every requirement issue to Product Owner.

If ownership is unknown, preserve that uncertainty.

If an item involves multiple teams, preserve shared ownership without duplicating the underlying finding.

No findings should disappear because of grouping.

8.2 Finding Presentation

Each finding should initially display only:

Title

A short, understandable description.

Problem

What is wrong or unclear?

Why it matters

Why should the team care?

What’s needed

What decision, clarification, or action is required?

Keep each section concise.

Avoid unnecessary paragraphs.

Example

Current technical wording:

“MM-04 — Chequebook fees switch controls fee options and amounts.”

Improved wording:

Should customers see cheque fees before confirming?

Problem: The requirements are unclear about when fees should appear.

Why it matters: Customers may place an order without knowing the fee.

What’s needed: Product Owner to confirm when fees must be displayed.

This is an illustration of simpler wording, not a new confirmed business requirement.

Do not invent information when simplifying findings.

8.3 Hide Technical Details

Move secondary information into expandable Show Details.

Include:

* Finding IDs.
* Verification status.
* Evidence.
* PRD references.
* Figma nodes.
* Code paths.
* Line numbers.
* Source report filenames.
* Original wording.
* Report status.

Preserve full traceability.

The simple presentation must not remove evidence.

8.4 Simplify Finding Statuses

Avoid repeating badges such as:

* Assumed.
* Requirements.
* Blocking.

Use minimal, meaningful user-facing states where appropriate:

* Needs decision.
* Needs clarification.
* Recommended change.
* Resolved.

Do not confuse:

* Severity.
* Verification confidence.
* Blocking state.
* Resolution state.

These must remain distinct in the underlying data.

Do not mark an item resolved merely because it was acknowledged.

Keep real blockers visible.

8.5 Clarifications Within Findings

Keep clarification questions in the Findings tab.

When a question requires attention:

* Navigate to Findings.
* Focus the specific question.
* Show a clear action.
* Allow the user to respond.
* Return to Overview after successful submission.

Avoid placing large, unrelated agent interaction cards above the findings list.

Workflow-level approvals should remain separate from normal findings, even when presented within the same workspace.

Do not misclassify approval gates as findings.

8.6 Filters and Counts

Provide simple team filters.

Show accurate counts.

Avoid large empty sections.

If a selected team has no findings, display a compact empty state.

Do not add unnecessary nested filters or dashboards.

8.7 Generated Team Messages

Apply the same simple-language principle to generated team messages.

Messages should clearly communicate:

1. What needs clarification or correction.
2. Why it matters.
3. What response is required.

Preserve exact technical references in supporting details.

Do not remove important evidence.

Do not change the business meaning.

8.8 Findings Data Integrity

Inspect the existing report templates and extraction logic.

Preserve:

* Stable finding ID.
* Original source content.
* Responsible team.
* Problem.
* Impact.
* Required action.
* Evidence.
* Verification classification.
* Blocking state.
* Resolution state when available.

Use existing metadata wherever possible.

Do not invent owners or conclusions.

Handle missing and malformed report sections gracefully.

Avoid unnecessary backend infrastructure.

